<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch08_P02_Euclid_number</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Follow Euclid's proof in a particular case. Suppose, for the sake of argument, that the only primes were {@ptxt@}. You may use a calculator.</p>
<ol class="tb-parts">
<li><p>Work out the number \(N\) that the proof uses.</p>
<div>\(N =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div></li>
<li><p>Give an example of a prime number that divides \(N\).</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div></li>
</ol>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Euclid's number is one more than the product of all the primes in the list: \[N = {@ev(apply("*", plist), simp=false)@} + 1 = {@NN@}.\]</p>
<p>Dividing \(N\) by any of {@ptxt@} leaves remainder 1, because each of them divides the product \({@Pp@}\). So none of them divides \(N\). But \(N\) is greater than 1, so some prime divides \(N\), and it cannot be in the list.</p>
<p>[[if test='primep(NN)']]Here \(N = {@NN@}\) is itself a new prime.[[else]]Here \(N\) is not prime: its prime factors are {@ptfac@}, each larger than {@pk@}.[[/if]]</p>
<p>The proof never claims that \(N\) is prime[[if test='is(kk < 6)']]: with the primes 2, 3, 5, 7, 11 and 13, \(N = 30031 = 59 \times 509\)[[/if]]. It only needs \(N\) to have a prime factor that is not in the list.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1000000</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026062900</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables><text><![CDATA[kk: rand([3, 4, 5, 6, 7]);
plist: firstn([2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17], kk);
pk: last(plist);
Pp: apply("*", plist);
NN: Pp + 1;
sumN: apply("+", plist) + 1;
nfac: map(first, ifactors(NN));
spf: first(nfac);
lpf: last(nfac);
ptfac: ["", "", "", "59 and 509", "19, 97 and 277"][kk - 2];
qnd: [7, 11, 13, 17, 23][kk - 2];
ptxt: ["2, 3 and 5", "2, 3, 5 and 7", "2, 3, 5, 7 and 11", "2, 3, 5, 7, 11 and 13", "2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13 and 17"][kk - 2];
s_assert(is(mod(NN, qnd) # 0), true);
s_assert(is(primep(NN) = is(kk < 6)), true);]]></text></questionvariables>
